# PR description template

This file tells the worker agent how to write the pull-request body for a
finished task. It is **instructions to the author**, not a
variable-substitution template — you (the worker) read it and write the
description yourself, because only an agent that understands the change can
produce a Mermaid diagram, an ASCII wireframe, or a real QA checklist.

Write the body **for the human reviewer**: someone who wants to know what
changed and why, not a copy of the task requirements. Ground every section in
what you actually built and the real problem it solves.

When you finish a task, write the PR body — following the sections below — to
`.shelbi/pr-body.md` in your worktree, then emit the ready marker as usual. If
you genuinely can't produce a useful description, don't write the file: Shelbi
falls back to a task-body-based PR so the handoff never breaks.

Emit these sections, in order, top to bottom. Omit a conditional section
entirely when it doesn't apply — don't leave an empty heading.

## 1. Summary

A few sentences on the feature or fix, grounded in the real problem it solves.
Pull the problem from the task's problem statement and pair it with what you
actually built. The reviewer should understand the *why* before the *how*.

## 2. Technical details

The things a reviewer should be aware of before reading the diff: key design
decisions and the alternatives you rejected, tradeoffs, risky or subtle areas,
and any migration or rollout notes. Call out anything that isn't obvious from
the code.

## 3. Data model changes (only if the change touches a database or schema)

If — and only if — the change adds, removes, or alters a database table,
column, index, or other persisted schema, include a Mermaid ER or class
diagram of the changed model. GitHub renders it natively:

````
```mermaid
erDiagram
    ORDER ||--o{ LINE_ITEM : contains
    ORDER {
        int id PK
        string status
    }
```
````

Show the changed entities and their relationships. Skip this section entirely
for a change with no schema/DB touch.

## 4. UI changes (only if the change alters the interface)

If the change alters a screen or component, show it:

- **Screenshots when you already have a shareable image** — a URL GitHub can
  reach, or an image committed on the branch you can reference by its raw URL.
- **Otherwise an ASCII wireframe** of the changed screen or component, so the
  reviewer can see the layout without running it. For example:

  ```
  +------------------------------------------+
  |  Settings                          [x]   |
  +------------------------------------------+
  |  [ ] Enable notifications                |
  |  [x] Dark mode                           |
  |                          [ Cancel ][ Save ]
  +------------------------------------------+
  ```

Automated screenshot upload is out of scope — use a wireframe when you have no
shareable image. Skip this section entirely for a non-UI change.

## 5. QA checklist

End every PR body with a QA checklist: concrete, checkable items a reviewer or
tester can run through to verify the change. Prefer specific, runnable steps
over generic ones. Use GitHub task-list syntax:

```
## QA checklist
- [ ] `cargo test --workspace` passes
- [ ] Open the settings dialog and confirm dark mode persists across restart
- [ ] Migration runs cleanly on an existing database
```
